  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nSurface high pressure that has made for pleasant conditions the\npast couple of days is slowly departing to the east. An upper\nlevel ridge is expected to slowly slide eastward and expand \nacross the Great Plains today. There is a fairly stout MCS in\neastern South Dakota, on the east side of the ridge axis that is\nslowly moving into southwestern Minnesota currently. The \nnorthwest flow from the ridge that is presently overhead would \nnormally steer this system directly to Chicago; however, models \nare suggesting a mid level wave to pass over the northern Plains\nwhich will help flatten the ridge. This will effectively help \nkeep most of the better forcing to the north of the Wisconsin \nstateline. Regional radar trends will be monitored through the \ncourse of the day to ensure that no southern deviation occurs, \nbut there is one other fly in the ointment which will help\nprevent concerns with rain/storm chances today: surface layer \ndry air. Event the most aggressive models that have the system \nmaking a southern shift and entering northern Illinois run into \nissues with the dry air that is currently over the region and\nshows the complex eroding away. For now, PoPs were constructed \nlimiting the chances to north of Interstate 88 this afternoon \ninto the early evening. There are certainly chances for \nsprinkles as south of Interstate 80, but confidence is low that \nthey can make it to the surface through the dry air. With the \nbetter forcing to the north, thunder is not currently forecasted\nfor this afternoon/evening time frame.\n\nA slightly better short wave is expected to ride over top of \nthe ridge tomorrow night and Sunday morning. Forcing with this\nwave will be slightly better over northern Illinois to provide \nbetter confidence to increase PoPs (relative to Saturday \nafternoon). Lapse rates look fairly poor leaving only narrow, \nskinny CAPE depicted in model soundings. Thunder mention was \nadded to the forecast, but limited the probability to less than\n25 percent as coverage is expected to be more isolated.\n\nThe previously mentioned upper level ridge will be responsible\nfor the weather for much of the week next week as it sits over \nmuch of the CONUS. There is uncertainty in the exact placement \nof the ridge, (more flat/southerly located = more \nshowers/storms; more amplified/centrally located = drier) to \nmerit confidence in timing and coverage through early next \nweek. No major changes deviated from the chance PoPs the NBM\nprovided. While periods of showers and storms will be possible,\nthe expectation is for far more drier times that wet.\n\nSouthwest flow will help advect warmer 850 mb temperatures \nunder the ridge through the week. Heat and humidity is expected \nto slowly rise through week. Models have been consistently \nshowing highest heat index values (in the mid to upper 90s) by \nthe middle of the week. The GFS is notably warmer than the than \nthe Euro, so there is still some uncertainty on the exact heat \nvalues, but there is gaining confidence in another hot stretch \nof temperatures as we enter September.\n\nAt the end of the forecast time period, models are trying to \nsuggest that the ridge starts to break down and returns \nnorthern Illinois to northwest flow overhead (which would aid\nshower/storm development). The NBM is clearly picking up on \nthis signal with higher end chance PoPs into next weekend. \nTiming at this distance is very fuzzy logic, but there may be \nbetter chances for rain/storms late Friday or Saturday. \n\nDK",
